<body>
<h1>Unit 5: Work, Energy and Power</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Energy is the bookkeeping device that survives every form of friction. The work-energy theorem is the most useful sentence in mechanics: it replaces a force problem with an energy problem, and it stays valid even when the forces themselves are hard to compute.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Work done by a constant force is the scalar product W = F.s.cos(theta); only the component of the force along the displacement contributes.</li><li>The work-energy theorem states that the net work on a particle equals its change in kinetic energy, dK = W_net, whatever the forces turn out to be.</li><li>A conservative force depends on position only; for it a potential energy exists with W = -dU, and the closed-path integral of the force vanishes.</li><li>Near the Earth's surface gravity is conservative with U = m g h; the spring force is conservative with U = (1/2) k x^2.</li><li>Mechanical energy conservation (K + U constant) holds only when no non-conservative work (friction, drag, driving) is done.</li><li>Power is the rate of energy transfer: P = dW/dt, and for a constant force P = F.v, so a bicycle climbs steeply not because it does more work but because it does it faster.</li><li>Work is a path-independent energy transfer only for conservative forces; friction turns ordered mechanical energy into disordered thermal energy, which is why the work-energy theorem has to carry a friction term.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Energy conservation is bookkeeping, not a claim that energy disappears: friction converts mechanical energy into internal energy and heats both surfaces.</li><li>Non-conservative forces cannot be described by a potential and must be handled through the work-energy theorem directly.</li><li>The constant-g potential m g h is a local approximation; the exact field potential is -GMm/r, and the two agree only for heights small compared with Earth's radius.</li><li>Efficiency is always defined against a specific output, can never exceed 1, and for most machines falls as the load rises because friction grows faster than useful work.</li><li>Power is a rate, not an amount: the same total energy can be delivered slowly or quickly, and the two are physically different because the time left to react is different.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Power of a typical classroom lamp</td><td>about 40 W</td></tr><tr><td>Energy in one kilogram of fat</td><td>about 37 MJ</td></tr><tr><td>Energy of 1 kWh</td><td>3.6 MJ</td></tr><tr><td>Efficiency of a modern electric motor</td><td>about 85 to 95 %</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How is the energy dissipated by friction partitioned between the two rubbing surfaces?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>The partition depends on temperature, speed and material pair; tribology still models it empirically rather than from first principles.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why does time run in one direction rather than both?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>The second law is statistical in the standard account, and the deeper question of why the universe began in a very low-entropy state is not settled by that account.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>A block slides down a rough incline: write the full energy budget and predict where it stops, including the heat produced.</li><li>Compare the stopping distance of a car braking from 20 m/s with and without a reaction delay, and explain the difference in energy terms.</li><li>Derive the efficiency of a block on a rough incline pushing a load uphill, then check the result against a direct work calculation.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Uncertainty/basic.html">NIST: Measurement uncertainty</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-3-work-energy-theorem">OpenStax: Work-Energy Theorem</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-4-power">OpenStax: Power</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
